\chapter{The native build helper and link plan}
\label{ch:67}
\index{The native build helper and link plan}

The single-file build helper runs \OnxCodeTerm{on1xc}, selects companion runtime and standard-library bridge sources, and uses the MuLIB plan for implementation kernels. A kernel group belongs in one implementation translation unit. Consumer and bridge files include declarations and link the implementation. Libraries with independently compiled providers or qualified native runtime companions must also be present in the link plan.

\section{Triage C build failures}
A generated C syntax or type error points at emission or a declaration mismatch. An undefined symbol at link time points at missing object placement, inaccessible static-inline linkage, or a signature/provider mismatch. A duplicate symbol can indicate the same implementation macro was defined in two translation units. Use emitted C, the helper's chosen plan, and a minimal C caller to isolate the layer. Supplying an Exotract archive is an alternate implementation source described in \cref{ch:94}. Consumer files should not reactivate the implementation macros from that archive.

\section{Read a JSON bridge request backwards}
Take a generated program with an extern declaration for
\OnxCodeTerm{on1x\_mulib\_json\_array\_length}. Start at the unresolved
reference in the program object. Its immediate provider is an On1x bridge,
not a MuLIB routine with the same name. The helper recognizes this spelling
in the generated externs, selects the JSON bridge group, and requests the
JSON kernel. The bridge and the implementation unit have different jobs:
one translates the call expected by the program; the other supplies the
kernel that the translation calls. Adding the kernel alone cannot supply
the missing bridge entry point.

Now imagine the program also uses the language's runtime JSON operations.
There are two reasons to request the same kernel, but there should still
be only one definition of its implementation. Trace the requests into the
planner before counting implementation objects. Counting JSON mentions in
the source, or even the headers included by the bridge, answers a different
question.

Request \OnxCodeTerm{--mulib-plan} with a report path separate from the
source and executable. In the resulting link report, compare the selected
bridge groups with the native recipe, then distinguish the fingerprints
of generated inputs from those of stable files. The executable digest
identifies the linked result. If a rebuild uses a different native compiler
but the same group names, check compiler identity and flags before attributing
a changed executable to the planner. None of those fields is an independent
review of the foreign function's behavior. For example, the report can
identify exactly which bridge was linked without saying which error result
the application is prepared to handle.

